From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 22:28:10 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(control): selection points are cell boundaries, not cell
 centres

Answering "is the off-by-one ours or ghostty's": ours. ghostty selects the
cell under the pointer, measured in the harness - a pointer at the start of
cell 5 selects cell 5.

What arrives at _mouseTo is a selection *boundary*, not a character index.
ControlInteractivity has already applied cascadia's midpoint rule before
calling: the click site passes round=true, commented "rounding can push the
position to the next cell", and the drag anchor nudges with termPos.x++ when
the drag runs leftwards to "place the anchor on the right side of the current
cell".

Sending the centre of that cell re-read the boundary as a character. Hence
both reported symptoms: selection started one character late whenever the
rounding had pushed the index up, and dragging extended the moment the
pointer touched the next character rather than at its midpoint - the midpoint
decision had already been made upstream and was being discarded here.

The x is now the cell edge. The y keeps its half-cell offset: rows have no
equivalent midpoint rule, and the centre is the unambiguous way to land in a
row.
---
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp        | 15 ++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
index 96ece4277..56bf56289 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
@@ -840,7 +840,20 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         // and got 5..8 back, and asked for 5..9 and got 11..14.
         const auto cellWidth = _cellSize.Width / _compositionScale;
         const auto cellHeight = _cellSize.Height / _compositionScale;
-        const auto x = (static_cast<double>(cell.X) + 0.5) * cellWidth;
+
+        // The cell edge, not its centre. What arrives here is a selection
+        // *boundary*, not a character index: ControlInteractivity has already
+        // applied cascadia's midpoint rule before calling, rounding the pointer
+        // to the nearest cell edge ("rounding can push the position to the next
+        // cell") and even nudging the anchor with termPos.x++ when a drag runs
+        // leftwards.
+        //
+        // Centring re-read that boundary as a character, which is why selection
+        // started one character late whenever the rounding had pushed the index
+        // up, and why dragging extended as soon as the pointer touched the next
+        // character instead of at its midpoint - the midpoint decision had
+        // already been made and was being thrown away.
+        const auto x = static_cast<double>(cell.X) * cellWidth;
         const auto y = (static_cast<double>(cell.Y) + 0.5) * cellHeight;
         ghostty_surface_mouse_pos(_surface, x, y, GHOSTTY_MODS_NONE);
     }
